WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.560
YURI GRINSHTEYN: We've seen
how to use OpenTelemetry

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:04.080
to instrument our services
for both distributed tracing

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:05.340
and custom metrics.

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:06.840
But what do we do
when tracing shows

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.390
us there's a latency issue
in a specific service?

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:11.220
That information
is often in logs.

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:13.950
So today, we'll go over how to
bring logs and traces together

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:16.230
into a single view,
using OpenTelemetry

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:17.730
and cloud trace and logging.

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:19.626
This is The Stack Doctor.

00:00:19.626 --> 00:00:22.542
[UPBEAT MUSIC]

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:28.800
Let's take a look at an
example app written in Go.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:31.650
It receives a user request,
does some internal processing,

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:34.733
then makes a back inquiry
before returning a response.

00:00:34.733 --> 00:00:36.150
Using OpenTelemetry,
we can create

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:38.370
trace bands that will
capture the timing of each

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:39.450
of those actions.

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:41.930
If the internal processing
step takes a long time,

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:44.603
we want to use logs written by
our app to diagnose further.

00:00:44.603 --> 00:00:46.770
The good news is that it's
possible to automatically

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:49.102
create associations
between traces and logs.

00:00:49.102 --> 00:00:51.060
We just have to the write
the idea of the trace

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:53.490
and span into the payload
of your log entries,

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:55.870
and cloud trace and
logging will do the rest.

00:00:55.870 --> 00:00:57.482
But what do you get those?

00:00:57.482 --> 00:00:59.940
Well, first, let's review some
basics about how distributed

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:01.860
tracing works in OpenTelemetry.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:04.360
You can refer to the official
documentation for more detail.

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:05.970
We'll link to that below.

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:08.640
A trace is essentially the
sum total of information

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:10.410
we want to collect
about the user request

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:12.650
as it traverses our
distributed system.

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:15.870
A span is information about
the request and response of one

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:17.730
of the services in our system.

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:19.702
Spans include span context.

00:01:19.702 --> 00:01:21.660
This is the information
that must be propagated

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:24.150
with every request that
allows the tracing library

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:26.550
to reconstruct the entire
trace when all requests are

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:27.700
completed.

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:30.090
Finally, Go provides
its own context

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:31.800
for request and
distributed systems

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:34.440
that contain information
about request propagation.

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:35.820
We'll use all of
this information

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:37.950
to identify our traces
and spans and connect

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:39.550
them to log entries.

00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:41.590
Now let's dive into our code.

00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:44.130
This is the function we'll
use to write our log entries.

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:45.480
There are two main parts here.

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.580
In the first part, we
extract the information

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:50.250
we need about the trace and
the span from the context.

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:51.900
Note that there
are two steps here.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:53.880
We get the span from
the request context,

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:56.430
then extract it's span
context, and finally

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:59.580
use that to get the
trace ID and span ID.

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:01.680
In the second part, we
use that information

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:03.420
to construct our log entry.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:06.180
We write the log payload and
add the trace and span ID

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:07.472
information to it.

00:02:07.472 --> 00:02:09.180
Here's what our log
entry looks like when

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:11.022
it's written to cloud logging.

00:02:11.022 --> 00:02:12.480
Notice that it
contains information

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:14.790
about the trace and span ID.

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:17.760
Finally, when we go to trace,
we can see that our trace now

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:18.690
includes log entries.

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:20.190
We can use them to
better understand

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:22.740
exactly what's happening if
one of the spans in the trace

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:24.480
shows a latency issue.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:26.870
Using OpenTelemetry to
connect traces with logs

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:29.680
is a great way to improve the
observability of our service

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:32.580
and make latency issues
much easier to debug.

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:33.820
That's it for this episode.

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:36.010
Thank you for watching, and
come back soon for more.

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:37.110
This is The Stack Doctor.

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:38.400
Stay healthy out there.

